 2. 🌊 Blue wave leaves climate behind 
 
 
 &nbsp; 
 
 Illustration: Aïda Amer/Axios. Stock: Getty Images Democrats made climate change a centerpiece of their campaigns for years — but they barely mentioned it this cycle, Axios' Amy Harder writes. 

 The midterms showed just how far the topic has fallen down the political priority list, even within the party most committed to addressing it. 🖼️ The big picture: Even some of the Democrats' most prominent climate advocates emphasized affordability over climate change — or reconsidered ambitious climate policies amid rising costs.

 🔎 Zoom in: New York Gov. Kathy Hochul, who once championed the state's ambitious climate agenda, pushed to weaken parts of its landmark climate law this year, citing affordability.

 Sen. Ed Markey (D-Mass.), who co-authored the Green New Deal, played down the proposal during his reelection campaign, emphasizing affordability and other priorities. Texas Sen.-elect James Talarico, who previously warned about fossil fuels' impact on the planet, campaigned on protecting oil and gas jobs alongside expanding renewable energy and lowering electricity bills. Keep reading . 

 
 
